================================================================
QR MATCHING CONFIDENCE UPGRADE PLAN
Goal : true target  -> confidence >= 0.90
       wrong target -> confidence <= 0.60
Status: PLANNED (not yet implemented)
================================================================

BASELINE PROBLEM
----------------
Confidence = module-grid bit agreement (29x29, best of 4 rotations),
optionally averaged with NCC. But every QR of the same version shares
fixed patterns (3 finder squares, separators, timing, alignment, format
info ~ 1/3 of all modules), so a WRONG code still scores ~0.60-0.75,
while a TRUE code caps at ~0.80-0.90 due to perspective/blur/sampling
loss. The two score distributions overlap -> no clean 0.90/0.60 split.

SECTION A - MAKE WRONG CODES SCORE LOWER (discriminability)
-----------------------------------------------------------
A1. Data-modules-only scoring (fixed-pattern mask)   [HIGHEST IMPACT]
    Compare agreement only on data + error-correction modules; skip the
    finder/timing/alignment/format regions shared by all QRs.
    False scores fall to ~0.50 (chance); true stays ~0.95+.
A2. Chance-corrected confidence
    Report (agreement - chance)/(1 - chance): random code ~ 0, perfect = 1.
A3. Version gate
    Estimate candidate module count; if version != template version the
    codes are provably different -> return confidence 0 immediately.
A4. Format-info pre-check
    Compare the 15-bit format region (mask pattern + EC level);
    mismatch = hard reject. Cheap and very discriminative.
A5. Metric gating instead of metric averaging
    Pixel-sim and NCC are correlated and both inflated by fixed patterns.
    Use module-grid agreement as primary score; NCC only as pass/fail
    gate (take min of the two, not the mean).

SECTION B - MAKE TRUE CODES SCORE HIGHER (geometric fidelity)
-------------------------------------------------------------
B1. Four-corner perspective warp                     [HIGH IMPACT]
    minAreaRect fixes rotation only, not keystone skew. Use the detector's
    4 corner points + homography to dewarp to a perfect square first.
B2. Module-centre sampling
    After warping to modules*k pixels, read each module at its centre (or
    small centre window) instead of full-cell block means (edge bleed).
B3. Rotate the grid, not the image
    Do the 4-rotation search on the integer module matrix: zero
    interpolation loss vs rotating pixel arrays.
B4. Warp-quality gate
    If corner reprojection error is high, mark frame "unstable" and skip
    scoring instead of emitting a mediocre confidence.

SECTION C - BINARIZATION FIDELITY
---------------------------------
C1. Module-scaled adaptive window
    Threshold window ~ 2-3 modules in pixels, not a fixed 11 px (which can
    sit inside one module and produce garbage thresholds).
C2. Sauvola / Niblack thresholding
    Handles shadows and uneven lighting better than adaptive Gaussian.
C3. Two-binarization consensus
    Binarize twice (e.g. Sauvola + Otsu); modules where they disagree are
    "unknown" and excluded from scoring. Raises true, denies false luck.
C4. Light morphological open/close
    Removes salt-and-pepper sensor noise before sampling.
C5. Keep template & candidate pipelines identical (already true - preserve).

SECTION D - CAPTURE-SIDE QUALITY
--------------------------------
D1. Minimum pixels-per-module gate (>= 5 px/module); smaller = no score.
D2. Shorter exposure + tighter sharpness gate (global shutter removes
    rolling artifacts but NOT motion blur).
D3. Reduce MJPG compression artifacts (YUYV/uncompressed if USB bandwidth
    allows at working resolution).
D4. Print & lighting control: matte print, no specular glare, even
    illumination, full quiet zone.
D5. Undistort before detection (already done - preserve).

SECTION E - SEMANTIC HARD GATES
-------------------------------
E1. Decode-oracle fallback                           [HIGH IMPACT]
    Decode the template image once at startup and store its text. Per
    frame, if a candidate decodes: strings equal -> confidence 1.0,
    different -> 0.0. Visual score used only when decode fails
    (blur/damage/extreme angle). Perfect separation when readable.
E2. Aspect & quiet-zone sanity gates before scoring
    (non-square candidate -> reject).

SECTION F - CALIBRATION & DECISION DESIGN
-----------------------------------------
F1. Labeled bench dataset
    Collect true & false candidate crops across distances/angles/lighting
    (candidate saves already exist in output tree).
F2. Score histograms + ROC / max-margin analysis
    Pick the operating point in the empty gap between the two clusters.
F3. Monotonic calibration curve (piecewise-linear or logistic)
    Maps raw score -> reported confidence so true lands > 0.90 and false
    < 0.60 by construction.
F4. Multi-frame evidence combination
    Combine per-frame likelihoods multiplicatively: sustained true view
    drives confidence to ~1.0; a single false flicker stays buried.

IMPLEMENTATION PRIORITY
-----------------------
1. A1 + B1 + B2  -> expect true ~0.85 -> ~0.97, false ~0.70 -> ~0.50
2. E1            -> perfect separation whenever the code is decodable
3. A3 + A4       -> instant zeros for wrong codes
4. F1 + F2 + F3  -> guarantees the 0.90 / 0.60 bands by measurement
5. C* + D*       -> removes the tail of bad true frames
6. A2, A5, B3, B4, E2, F4 -> polish & robustness

EXPECTED OUTCOME AFTER STEPS 1-4
--------------------------------
Score distributions no longer overlap:
  true  >= 0.90   (typically 0.95 - 0.99)
  false <= 0.60   (typically 0.45 - 0.55, or hard 0.0 via gates)
